<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Earhart is a 25-year-old private pilot who comes for her yearly preventive visit four weeks after her first visit for chronic sinus symptoms. Chronic rhinosinusitis without polyps was confirmed by nasal endoscopy two weeks ago, during a flare with pus on the left. She finished ten days of amoxicillin-clavulanate five days ago; the culture grew Haemophilus influenzae that makes beta-lactamase and is sensitive to amoxicillin-clavulanate. The discolored drainage has stopped and the left cheek pressure is less, but her nose is still blocked most days and her sense of smell has not come back. She rinses with saline twice daily and uses the fluticasone spray every morning, with no nosebleeds. She has not flown since the endoscopy, and she stopped the nighttime cold tablet. Her SNOT-22 today is 44, from 52 at the first visit and 58 during the flare. Nobody from the clinic called her at the end of the antibiotic course, as had been planned; she would have called if she had been worse. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 25</td></tr><tr><td>Sinuses</td><td>Antibiotic course finished; drainage stopped; still blocked; smell reduced; SNOT-22 44</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Saline irrigation twice daily and fluticasone daily, correct technique</td></tr><tr><td>Flying</td><td>Grounded herself since the endoscopy; no-go night tablet stopped</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-2 0</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on a tablet in the waiting room and reviewed with her by Dr. Talbot. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good apart from her sinuses</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Lives alone; close to her sister and to friends at the airfield; frustrated at missing weekend flights</td></tr><tr><td>Substance use</td><td>AUDIT-C 0; no alcohol, cannabis, or other drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never smoked; no vaping</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks or cycles 30 minutes most days; swims in summer</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Regular meals; fruit and vegetables daily</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Seven hours, broken by nasal blockage two or three nights a week</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual and reproductive health</td><td>No partner at present; copper intrauterine device placed two years ago; not planning a pregnancy</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and noise</td><td>Uses a noise-reducing headset in the cockpit; no hearing difficulty</td></tr><tr><td>Sun exposure</td><td>Long hours in a sunny cockpit; uses sunscreen inconsistently</td></tr><tr><td>Safety at home</td><td>Feels safe; no one hurts, threatens, or controls her; no firearm in the home</td></tr><tr><td>Road safety</td><td>Wears a seat belt; a shoulder harness in the aircraft</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

